  <div class="title">
    <h1>Mosthofa Imran: Systems, Arguments,<br>and Known Failure Modes</h1>
    <div class="sub">A living document. Revised in place, never quietly. Section 7 records where it was wrong.</div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="status">Status of This Memo</h2>
  <div class="ind">
    <div class="memo">This document borrows the form of an IETF Internet-Draft. It is not one, it has no
      standing with the IETF, and nothing here was reviewed by anybody but the author. The form
      is used because a working document that expires unless revised is the right shape for the
      argument this site is making, not to borrow authority.
      <br><br>
      This memo provides information about one engineer. It does not specify a standard of
      any kind, and its normative language in Section 4 binds nobody but the author. Distribution is
      unlimited. It is <b>not</b> a résumé; it is the record from which a résumé would be an abridgement.
      Systems appear with the numbers they produced. Arguments appear with a confidence value and the
      conditions under which the author would abandon them. Section 6 is access controlled.
      Corrections are published in Section 7 with credit to the person who sent them.</div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="abstract">Abstract</h2>
  <div class="ind prose">
    <p>Eleven years of production engineering. I design multi-tenant agentic systems that ship from a single
    build into public cloud and into air-gapped data centres inside regulated banks: active-active across two
    sites, tens of millions of events a day, recovery objectives measured in minutes because the alternative
    is a conversation with a regulator.</p>
    <p>I also write. The running argument is that this industry has learned to manufacture the feeling of
    competence faster than it can produce the thing itself, and that a good deal of what is sold as developer
    experience is friction relocated to a worse hour. Section 5 states those claims in a form that can be
    attacked, and Section 7 lists the ones that were.</p>
  </div>

